var CONTENT={"1.1": {"h": "<p><b>Data science<\/b> is the study of data that helps us derive useful insight for decisions. It is a multidisciplinary approach that combines mathematics, statistics, artificial intelligence and computer engineering to analyse large amounts of data and answer: what happened, why it happened, what will happen, and what can be done about it.<\/p>\n<h4>Components<\/h4>\n<p>Statistics, visualisation, data engineering, domain experts, machine learning and deep learning (a subset of machine learning based on neural networks).<\/p>\n<h4>Applications<\/h4>\n<ul><li><b>Healthcare:<\/b> disease prediction, personalised medicine.<\/li><li><b>Finance:<\/b> fraud and risk detection, investment patterns.<\/li><li><b>E-commerce and media:<\/b> recommendations (Amazon, YouTube), targeted advertising, price comparison.<\/li><li><b>Search, speech and images:<\/b> Google, Alexa and Siri, Facebook image recognition.<\/li><li><b>Transport and logistics:<\/b> airline route planning, delivery (DHL, FedEx), automotive predictive maintenance and self-driving.<\/li><li><b>Education:<\/b> predicting performance and drop-out, personalised learning. Also gaming, AR, robots and chatbots.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>The life cycle (process)<\/h4>\n<img class=\"fig\" src=\"img/lifecycle.png\" alt=\"data science life cycle\">\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Stage<\/th><th>What happens<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Discovery / data collection<\/td><td>understand the problem; gather data (web logs, social media, census, APIs, IoT sensors)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Data preparation<\/td><td>clean (missing values, blank columns, wrong formats), transform (normalise), feature engineering<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Model planning<\/td><td>descriptive statistics, visualisation, correlation analysis; choose techniques<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Model building<\/td><td>split into training and testing sets; regression, classification, clustering; test the model<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Operationalise<\/td><td>deploy with reports, code and APIs; monitor performance<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Communicate results<\/td><td>present findings to stakeholders; decide success or failure<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p><b>How to answer an \"explain the life cycle for this case\" question (8 marks):<\/b> list the stages in order, and for each one say what it means <i>for the given case<\/i> (what data, what cleaning, which model, what metric). Draw the diagram. Examples: the PowerGrid energy-demand case and the recommender cases.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q1", "Nov25 Q11", "Nov25 Q17a", "Nov24 Q17a", "QA Q4", "Apr26 Q1", "Apr26 Q17a", "Apr26 Q17b", "Apr24 Q1", "Apr24 Q2", "Apr24 Q17b"]}, "1.2": {"h": "<p><b>Data<\/b> is raw facts; <b>information<\/b> is data that has been processed so it is useful. More than 120 zettabytes of data were generated in 2023. Sources: social media, machine/IoT data, transactions, healthcare, government and public data, media and entertainment, industrial and scientific research data.<\/p>\n<h4>Structured, semi-structured, unstructured<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th><\/th><th>Structured<\/th><th>Semi-structured<\/th><th>Unstructured<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Form<\/td><td>fixed model, tables<\/td><td>tags/markers, self-describing<\/td><td>no model<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Stored in<\/td><td>relational DB, Excel (SQL)<\/td><td>JSON, XML files<\/td><td>NoSQL, files<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Examples<\/td><td>names, dates, transactions<\/td><td>JSON, XML<\/td><td>emails, images, video, tweets<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Over 80% of data is unstructured.<\/p>\n<h4>Qualitative (categorical) vs quantitative (numerical)<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th><\/th><th>Categorical<\/th><th>Numerical<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Describes<\/td><td>quality, category<\/td><td>quantity, measurable<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Subtypes<\/td><td>nominal (no order: blood group), ordinal (ordered: Small, Medium, Large)<\/td><td>discrete (counted: number of pencils), continuous (measured: weight)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Graphs<\/td><td>bar chart, pie chart<\/td><td>histogram, scatter plot, line graph, box plot<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Quick test: temperature every hour is continuous; number of unread messages is discrete; T-shirt size is ordinal; nationality is nominal; a YouTube comment is unstructured; an ATM transaction is structured.<\/p>\n<h4>Repositories<\/h4>\n<p>A data repository is a library of datasets: Kaggle, UCI Machine Learning Repository, Google Dataset Search, Data.gov, World Bank Open Data, OpenML, AWS Open Data. Sources are internal databases, external public datasets, data warehouses and lakes, APIs and web scraping.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov24 Q1", "Nov24 Q3", "Nov24 Q11", "QA Q1", "QA Q3", "Apr24 Q11"]}, "1.3": {"h": "<h4>Big data: the 5 Vs<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>V<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Volume<\/td><td>amount of data to process<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Variety<\/td><td>number of data types (structured, semi-structured, unstructured)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Velocity<\/td><td>speed at which data is generated, collected and analysed<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Veracity<\/td><td>quality and trustworthiness; inconsistency and uncertainty<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Value<\/td><td>data is useful only when turned into insight<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p><b>Big data vs small data:<\/b> big data is the large picture, mostly unstructured; small data is focused, structured and easily interpreted.<\/p>\n<h4>Python toolbox<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Library<\/th><th>Use<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>NumPy<\/td><td>arrays and maths<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Pandas<\/td><td>DataFrames: read, clean, group, summarise<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Matplotlib, Seaborn<\/td><td>plots<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>SciPy, Statsmodels<\/td><td>statistics, tests, clustering<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Scikit-learn<\/td><td>machine learning and metrics<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>TensorFlow, PyTorch<\/td><td>deep learning<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Other tools: SQL, R, SAS, Power BI, Tableau, Jupyter/Colab.<\/p>\n<h4>Challenges of data science<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Difficult access to data, and poor data quality (the biggest problem).<\/li><li>Integrating data of different formats and standards.<\/li><li>Massive datasets need heavy computing.<\/li><li>Lack of domain experts; explaining results to non-technical people.<\/li><li>Data security and privacy.<\/li><\/ul>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q2", "Nov25 Q17b", "Nov24 Q2", "Nov24 Q17b", "Apr24 Q17a"]}, "2.1": {"h": "<p>Pandas holds tabular data in a <b>DataFrame<\/b> (a table of labelled columns) and a <b>Series<\/b> (one column). The commands that appear in every exam:<\/p>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Task<\/th><th>Command<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>create<\/td><td><code>pd.DataFrame({'a': [1, 2], 'b': [3, 4]})<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>import<\/td><td><code>pd.read_csv('f.csv')<\/code>, with <code>usecols=[...]<\/code>, <code>na_values=['NA']<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>look<\/td><td><code>head(n)<\/code>, <code>tail(n)<\/code>, <code>shape<\/code>, <code>columns<\/code>, <code>dtypes<\/code>, <code>index<\/code>, <code>size<\/code>, <code>info()<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>statistics<\/td><td><code>describe()<\/code> (numbers), <code>describe(include='all')<\/code>, <code>describe(include='object')<\/code> (categories), <code>value_counts()<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>select columns<\/td><td><code>df['a']<\/code>, <code>df[['a', 'b']]<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>select by position<\/td><td><code>df.iloc[2:6, 0]<\/code>: rows 2 to 5, first column (end excluded)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>select by label<\/td><td><code>df.loc[rows, cols]<\/code>, e.g. <code>df.loc[df['dept'] == 'CSE', ['name', 'cgpa']]<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>filter<\/td><td><code>df[df['age'] &gt; 25]<\/code>; several conditions: <code>df[(df['age'] &gt; 25) &amp; (df['genre'] == 'Female')]<\/code>; <code>df.query('age &gt; 25 and cgpa &gt; 8')<\/code>; <code>between(50, 60)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>new column<\/td><td><code>df['total'] = df['a'] + df['b']<\/code>; <code>df['grade'] = df['t'].apply(lambda m: 'A' if m &gt;= 85 else 'B')<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>add a row<\/td><td><code>df.loc[len(df)] = [...]<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>rename, drop<\/td><td><code>rename(columns={'old': 'new'})<\/code>, <code>drop(columns='a', inplace=True)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>sort, rank<\/td><td><code>sort_values(by='a', ascending=False)<\/code>, <code>df['a'].rank()<\/code>, <code>sort_index()<\/code>, <code>reset_index(drop=True)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>group<\/td><td><code>df.groupby('dept')['marks'].mean()<\/code>, <code>.size()<\/code>, <code>.agg([...])<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>sum, mean<\/td><td><code>df['s'].sum()<\/code>, <code>mean()<\/code>, <code>median()<\/code>, <code>duplicated()<\/code><\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p><b>How to write these answers:<\/b> number the sub-steps as in the question, one or two lines of code each, with a short comment; when the question shows a table, create the DataFrame from it first (NaN where the table shows NaN). Every answer for these questions in this tutor was run for real and shows its output.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q4", "Nov25 Q18", "Nov24 Q4", "Nov24 Q18a", "QA Q2", "QA Q5", "PD Q1", "PD Q2", "PD Q3", "PD Q4"]}, "2.2": {"h": "<p>A <b>missing value<\/b> is stored as <code>NaN<\/code> (\"not a number\") or <code>None<\/code>. Find them with <code>df.info()<\/code>, <code>df.isnull()<\/code> and <code>df.isnull().sum()<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h4>Why they matter<\/h4>\n<ul><li>They bias statistics (mean, correlation) and can give a wrongly biased model.<\/li><li>Most machine learning algorithms cannot take NaN.<\/li><li>Dropping rows or columns blindly loses valuable information.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Why they occur<\/h4>\n<p>Data corruption, incomplete data entry, human error, users deliberately skipping sensitive questions, survey non-response.<\/p>\n<h4>Handling<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Method<\/th><th>Command<\/th><th>When<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Drop rows<\/td><td><code>df.dropna()<\/code> / <code>dropna(how='any', subset=['col'])<\/code><\/td><td>few values missing<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Drop columns<\/td><td><code>df.dropna(axis=1)<\/code><\/td><td>column mostly empty<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Constant<\/td><td><code>df.fillna(0)<\/code><\/td><td>rarely right: 0 may look like data<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Mean<\/td><td><code>df['c'].fillna(df['c'].mean())<\/code><\/td><td>symmetric numeric data<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Median<\/td><td><code>fillna(df['c'].median())<\/code><\/td><td>skewed numeric data<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Mode<\/td><td><code>fillna(df['c'].mode()[0])<\/code><\/td><td>categorical data<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Group mean<\/td><td><code>groupby('g')['c'].transform('mean')<\/code><\/td><td>same class as the tuple<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Previous / next<\/td><td><code>ffill()<\/code> / <code>bfill()<\/code> (<code>fillna(method='ffill')<\/code>)<\/td><td>ordered data<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Interpolation<\/td><td><code>interpolate(method='linear')<\/code><\/td><td>sequence or time series<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Model<\/td><td><code>SimpleImputer<\/code>, <code>KNNImputer(n_neighbors=3)<\/code>, regression/logistic regression<\/td><td>most probable value<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Other cleaning steps: remove duplicates, fix data types, fix inconsistent spellings, treat outliers, normalise.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q12", "Nov25 Q21a", "Apr26 Q11", "Apr26 Q24a", "Apr24 Q12"]}, "2.3": {"h": "<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Plot<\/th><th>Use it for<\/th><th>Data<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Bar chart<\/td><td>compare categories<\/td><td>categorical vs a number<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Pie chart<\/td><td>parts of a whole<\/td><td>categorical<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Histogram<\/td><td>shape of a distribution (skew, spread, outliers)<\/td><td>one numeric<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Box plot<\/td><td>five-number summary, median, IQR, outliers<\/td><td>one numeric (by group)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Scatter plot<\/td><td>relationship and correlation between two variables<\/td><td>two numeric<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Line graph<\/td><td>trend over time<\/td><td>numeric over time<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Heat map<\/td><td>correlation matrix<\/td><td>many numeric<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nplt.bar(df['dept'], df['students'])      # bar chart\nplt.hist(df['marks'], bins=5)            # histogram\nplt.scatter(df['hours'], df['marks'])    # scatter plot\nplt.boxplot(df['marks'])                 # box plot\nplt.xlabel('x'); plt.ylabel('y'); plt.title('title'); plt.show()<\/pre>\n<h4>Exploratory data analysis (EDA)<\/h4>\n<p>EDA is looking at the data before modelling: understand columns and types, clean it, describe it with statistics, study one variable (histogram, box plot), then relationships (scatter plot, correlation), detect outliers, and decide on features and the model. Visualisation shows distribution, relationships and outliers at a glance and communicates results to non-technical readers.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q3", "Nov24 Q12", "Nov24 Q18b", "Apr26 Q7", "Apr26 Q15", "Apr26 Q18a", "Apr24 Q3", "Apr24 Q18"]}, "3.1": {"h": "<h4>Central tendency<\/h4>\n<ul><li><b>Mean<\/b> = sum / n. Affected by outliers.<\/li><li><b>Median<\/b> = middle value of the sorted data (average of the two middle values when n is even).<\/li><li><b>Mode<\/b> = most frequent value (there can be none, one or several).<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>Symmetric data: mean is about the median. Right-skewed: mean &gt; median. Left-skewed: mean &lt; median.<\/p>\n<h4>Spread<\/h4>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">variance   sigma² = Σ (x - mean)² / N      (population);  s² = Σ (x - mean)² / (n - 1)  (sample)\nstd dev    σ = √(variance)\nrange      max - min\nIQR        Q3 - Q1<\/pre>\n<p>Variance is the average of the squared differences from the mean; the standard deviation is its square root, in the units of the data.<\/p>\n<h4>Five-number summary and IQR (the teacher's method)<\/h4>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Sort the data.<\/li><li><b>Median (Q2)<\/b> = middle value.<\/li><li><b>Q1<\/b> = median of the data points to the <b>left<\/b> of the median (lower half).<\/li><li><b>Q3<\/b> = median of the data points to the <b>right<\/b> of the median (upper half).<\/li><li>Minimum and maximum are the smallest and largest values. IQR = Q3 - Q1.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>When n is odd the median itself is left out of both halves. (Software such as numpy interpolates and can give slightly different quartiles; in the exam use the halves method.)<\/p>\n<h4>Box plot<\/h4>\n<p>Draw a box from Q1 to Q3 with a line at the median, and whiskers to the minimum and maximum. Outlier fences: lower = Q1 - 1.5 IQR, upper = Q3 + 1.5 IQR; points beyond a fence are plotted separately as outliers and the whiskers stop at the last point inside.<\/p>\n<img class=\"fig\" src=\"img/n25_q13.png\" alt=\"box plot example\">\n<p>Worked: 18, 27, 34, 52, 54, 59, 61, 68, 78, 82, 85, 87, 91, 93, 100 gives Q1 = 52, median = 68, Q3 = 87, IQR = 35 (see Nov25 Q13).<\/p>\n<h4>z-score<\/h4>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">z = (x - mean) / sd         how many standard deviations one value is from the mean<\/pre>\n<p>Example: mean 60, SD 10, mark 80 gives z = 2. Standard deviation says how spread out the values are; the standard error says how reliable the sample mean is.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q6", "Nov25 Q13", "Nov24 Q5", "Nov24 Q6", "Apr26 Q5", "Apr26 Q12", "Apr26 Q18b", "Apr24 Q4", "Apr24 Q14"]}, "3.2": {"h": "<p><b>Estimation<\/b> infers a population value from a sample. <b>Point estimation<\/b> gives a single value (the sample mean is the point estimate of the population mean). <b>Interval estimation<\/b> gives a range likely to contain the population parameter: a <b>confidence interval<\/b>.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">standard error     SE = s / √(n)         (good estimate when n &gt; 30)\nconfidence interval   x̄ ± Z x s / √(n)\nZ = 1.645 for 90%, 1.96 for 95%, 2.576 for 99% confidence<\/pre>\n<p>Teacher's example: 97 women, s = 64.9, mean cholesterol 261.75, 95%: SE = 64.9 / sqrt(97) = 6.59, margin = 1.96 x 6.59 = 12.92, so the interval is (248.83, 274.67).<\/p>\n<p>Outliers affect the mean; remove them or use the median. Other ways to measure the variability of an estimate: the sampling distribution of the mean, the empirical standard error, and <b>bootstrapping<\/b> (resampling the sample with replacement many times).<\/p>\n<p>Population = all members; sample = part of the population used to describe it; a statistic describes a sample, a parameter describes the population.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q19b"]}, "3.3": {"h": "<h4>Covariance and correlation<\/h4>\n<ul><li><b>Covariance<\/b> says whether two variables move together (positive), oppositely (negative) or not related (zero); it ranges from -infinity to +infinity and depends on units.<\/li><li><b>Correlation<\/b> measures how strongly two variables are related; it ranges from -1 to +1. Both only measure <b>linear<\/b> relationships, and correlation is not causation.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Pearson correlation<\/h4>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">r = Σ[(x - x̄)(y - ȳ)] / √( Σ(x - x̄)² x Σ(y - ȳ)² )  =  covariance / (sd_x x sd_y)<\/pre>\n<p>Table to draw: x, y, x - xbar, y - ybar, product, (x - xbar)^2, (y - ybar)^2, each with a total. Then substitute.<\/p>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>r<\/th><th>Interpretation<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>0.8 to 1<\/td><td>very strong positive<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.6 to 0.8<\/td><td>strong positive<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.4 to 0.6<\/td><td>moderate<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0.2 to 0.4<\/td><td>weak<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>0 to 0.2<\/td><td>very weak or none<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Negative values read the same way, with the variables moving in opposite directions. Pearson does not work well with outliers and misses non-linear relationships.<\/p>\n<h4>Spearman rank correlation<\/h4>\n<p>Measures a <b>monotonic<\/b> relationship using ranks; use it when the relation is non-linear, the data is ordinal, there are outliers, or ranks are given.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">ρ = 1 - 6 Σ d² / ( n (n² - 1) ),      d = rank of x - rank of y<\/pre>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Given X and Y (ranks may already be given).<\/li><li>If not given, rank both: smallest = 1.<\/li><li><b>Ties:<\/b> values that repeat share the average of the ranks they occupy (two 67s at ranks 6 and 7 both get 6.5).<\/li><li>Find d and d^2 and total d^2.<\/li><li>Substitute into rho and interpret.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>Pearson vs Spearman: actual values vs ranks; linear vs monotonic; sensitive vs robust to outliers.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q19a", "Nov24 Q19a", "Nov24 Q19b", "Apr26 Q3", "REV Q1", "PP Q2", "PP Q3", "PS Q1", "PS Q2", "PS Q3", "Apr24 Q13"]}, "3.4": {"h": "<p>A <b>hypothesis<\/b> is a claim about a population tested with sample data. Two competing statements:<\/p>\n<ul><li><b>H0, null hypothesis:<\/b> assumed true until the evidence says otherwise; it always contains the equality (=, &lt;= or &gt;=).<\/li><li><b>H1, alternative hypothesis:<\/b> contradicts H0 (&lt;, &gt; or not equal).<\/li><\/ul>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Claim wording<\/th><th>H0<\/th><th>H1<\/th><th>Test<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>\"is different from / no longer equals 5 g\"<\/td><td>mu = 5<\/td><td>mu &ne; 5<\/td><td>two-tailed<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>\"greater than / overfilled / improved\"<\/td><td>mu = (or &lt;=) value<\/td><td>mu &gt; value<\/td><td>right-tailed<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>\"less than / too high\"<\/td><td>mu = (or &gt;=) value<\/td><td>mu &lt; value<\/td><td>left-tailed<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>If the claim itself contains the equality (for example \"at least 130\" or \"no longer than 10\"), the claim is H0.<\/p>\n<h4>The Z-test (population sigma known, or n large)<\/h4>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>State H0 and H1 and the significance level alpha (5% typical).<\/li><li>Find the critical value: right- or left-tailed 1.645 at 5%; two-tailed 1.96 at 5%.<\/li><li>Compute <code>z = (xbar - mu) / (sigma / sqrt(n))<\/code>.<\/li><li>Decide: if z falls in the rejection region (beyond the critical value) <b>reject H0<\/b>; otherwise do not reject.<\/li><li>Write the conclusion in words.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>Worked: n = 50, xbar = 7.2, mu = 6.5, sigma = 1.5 gives z = 3.30 &gt; 1.645, so reject H0 (Nov24 Q13).<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q5", "Nov24 Q13", "Apr26 Q8", "Apr24 Q5"]}, "4.1": {"h": "<p><b>Machine learning<\/b> is the ability of a machine to learn automatically from past data without being explicitly programmed for every rule. A system has two phases: <b>training<\/b> and <b>testing<\/b>. Every real-world problem falls into one of three types:<\/p>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Type<\/th><th>Data<\/th><th>Tasks<\/th><th>Algorithms<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Supervised<\/b><\/td><td>labelled<\/td><td>classification (category), regression (number)<\/td><td>logistic regression, KNN, decision tree; simple, multiple, polynomial regression<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Unsupervised<\/b><\/td><td>unlabelled<\/td><td>clustering, association<\/td><td>K-means, agglomerative clustering; recommendation / \"X implies Y\" rules<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Reinforcement<\/b><\/td><td>rewards and penalties<\/td><td>learn a policy by trial and error<\/td><td>game agents, robots, self-driving<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<h4>Recognise the type from the story<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Examples with a <b>known label<\/b> for each record (spam / not spam, disease / no disease, a price): <b>supervised<\/b>. Label is a category: classification; label is a number: regression.<\/li><li>\"Groups not known in advance\", \"discovers patterns\", \"no predefined categories\": <b>unsupervised<\/b> (clustering; or association if it finds items bought together).<\/li><li><b>Rewards and penalties<\/b>, \"learns by trying\", a game or a robot: <b>reinforcement<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q20b", "Nov24 Q22b", "Apr26 Q2", "Apr26 Q4", "Apr26 Q6", "PP Q7", "PP Q8", "PP Q9", "PP Q10", "PP Q11", "PP Q12", "PP Q13", "PP Q14", "PP Q15", "PP Q16", "Apr24 Q7", "Apr24 Q8", "Apr24 Q10", "Apr24 Q20b", "Apr24 Q23b"]}, "4.2": {"h": "<p><b>Regression<\/b> builds a prediction model y = f(x) that estimates a response from inputs. Independent variable (x, predictor, feature) and dependent variable (y, target).<\/p>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Type<\/th><th>Equation<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Simple linear<\/td><td>y = b0 + b1 x<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Multiple linear<\/td><td>y = b0 + b1 x1 + b2 x2 + ... + bn xn<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Polynomial<\/td><td>y = b0 + b1 x + b2 x^2 + ... + bk x^k (non-linear regression; a curve)<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<h4>Ordinary least squares: how to solve a numeric problem<\/h4>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">b1 = Σ (x - x̄)(y - ȳ) / Σ (x - x̄)²\nb0 = ȳ - b1 x̄\nresidual  e = y - y_hat  (actual - predicted)<\/pre>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Find xbar and ybar.<\/li><li>Draw the table: x, y, x - xbar, y - ybar, product, (x - xbar)^2 with totals.<\/li><li>b1 = total product / total of squares; b0 = ybar - b1 xbar.<\/li><li>Write the line y = b0 + b1 x; predict by substituting x.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p>Teacher's worked example: x = 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 and y = 60, 75, 85, 90, 92: xbar = 4, ybar = 80.4, b1 = 79 / 10 = 7.9, b0 = 48.8, so y = 48.8 + 7.9x; at x = 8, y = 112.<\/p>\n<img class=\"fig\" src=\"img/pp_q5.png\" alt=\"regression line example\">\n<p>Predicting far outside the data range (extrapolation) is unreliable.<\/p>\n<h4>Error metrics<\/h4>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">MSE  = (1/N) Σ (y - y_hat)²        RMSE = √(MSE)        MAE = (1/N) Σ |y - y_hat|\nR²  = 1 - Σ (y - y_hat)² / Σ (y - ȳ)²      (closer to 1 = better fit)<\/pre>\n<p>Multiple vs polynomial regression: several different inputs vs powers of one input.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q7", "Nov25 Q22b", "Nov24 Q14", "Nov24 Q23a", "Nov24 Q23b", "Apr26 Q19a", "Apr26 Q19b", "PP Q1", "PP Q5", "PP Q6", "Apr24 Q15", "Apr24 Q19", "Apr24 Q21"]}, "4.3": {"h": "<p>Despite its name, <b>logistic regression is a classification algorithm<\/b>. It predicts a categorical outcome (Yes/No, 0/1) as a probability. A straight line cannot be used for that, so the linear equation is passed through the <b>sigmoid<\/b> (S-curve) function that squeezes any value into the range 0 to 1; a threshold (usually 0.5) decides the class.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^-z),    z = b0 + b1 x       class 1 if p ≥ 0.5<\/pre>\n<img class=\"fig\" src=\"img/sigmoid.png\" alt=\"sigmoid curve\">\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th><\/th><th>Linear regression<\/th><th>Logistic regression<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Predicts<\/td><td>continuous value<\/td><td>probability of a category<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Problem<\/td><td>regression<\/td><td>classification<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Curve<\/td><td>straight line<\/td><td>S-shaped sigmoid<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Estimation<\/td><td>least squares<\/td><td>maximum likelihood<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Example: will an employee be promoted from the performance rating? Output near 1 means likely promoted, near 0 unlikely.<\/p>", "qs": ["Apr26 Q13", "Apr24 Q19"]}, "4.4": {"h": "<p><b>K-nearest neighbours<\/b> is a simple supervised algorithm (classification mainly, also regression). It is a <b>lazy<\/b> learner (it memorises the training data) and <b>non-parametric<\/b> (no assumption about the data). It assumes that similar things are near each other.<\/p>\n<h4>Procedure<\/h4>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Choose K (a positive integer, usually odd, e.g. 3).<\/li><li>Compute the distance from the new point to every training point: <code>d = sqrt((x1 - a1)^2 + (x2 - a2)^2 + ...)<\/code> (Euclidean; Manhattan and Minkowski are other options).<\/li><li>Sort the distances in ascending order.<\/li><li>Take the K nearest points.<\/li><li>Count the classes among them and choose the <b>majority vote<\/b>.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p><b>Layout for the answer:<\/b> a table with one row per training point (features, class, squared differences, distance, rank), then the K nearest and the vote, then one line: \"the new point is classified as ...\".<\/p>\n<ul><li>There is no rule for K: a larger K reduces the effect of noise; try several.<\/li><li>Slow for big data; features on larger scales dominate the distance, so scale them (min-max) when units differ a lot.<\/li><\/ul>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Worked (Nov25 Q22a)<\/th><th>New movie (7.0, 90)<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>3 nearest<\/td><td>C (10.03, Must Watch), B (30.02, Skip), A (40.03, Must Watch)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Vote<\/td><td>Must Watch 2, Skip 1<\/td><\/tr><\/table>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q22a", "Nov24 Q20", "PP Q4", "KT Q1", "KT Q2"]}, "4.5": {"h": "<p>A <b>decision tree<\/b> is a supervised model (CART, used for classification and regression) in the shape of a flow chart. <b>Root node<\/b>: first split on the most significant predictor. <b>Internal node<\/b>: a decision on a predictor. <b>Branch<\/b>: an outcome (yes/no or value). <b>Leaf<\/b>: the final class.<\/p>\n<h4>ID3: entropy and information gain (log base 2)<\/h4>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">Entropy(S) = - Σ p_i log2 p_i         0 = pure, 1 = two classes equally mixed\nGain(S, A) = Entropy(S) - Σ (|Sv|/|S|) Entropy(Sv)       choose the attribute with the highest gain<\/pre>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Entropy of the parent (target variable).<\/li><li>For every predictor: entropy of each branch, weighted average, information gain.<\/li><li>The highest gain becomes the root.<\/li><li>For each branch repeat with the remaining predictors until the data is correctly classified.<\/li><\/ol>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Play golf (14 days)<\/th><th>Gain<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Outlook<\/td><td><b>0.247<\/b><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Humidity<\/td><td>0.152<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Windy<\/td><td>0.048<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Temperature<\/td><td>0.029<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<img class=\"fig\" src=\"img/dt_golf.png\" alt=\"decision tree for play golf\">\n<p><b>Advantages:<\/b> easy to understand, handles numeric and categorical data, no feature scaling, handles non-linearity. <b>Disadvantages:<\/b> many layers become complex, <b>overfitting<\/b> (fix by <b>pruning<\/b> or a random forest), high variance and instability.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov24 Q7", "Nov24 Q22a", "Apr26 Q21a", "Apr26 Q21b", "Apr24 Q6", "Apr24 Q20a"]}, "4.6": {"h": "<p><b>Clustering<\/b> is unsupervised learning: group similar objects so that objects within a cluster are similar (high intra-cluster similarity) and objects in different clusters are different. Flat (partitioning) methods: K-means, fuzzy c-means. Hierarchical: agglomerative, divisive. <b>Hard<\/b> clustering puts every object in exactly one cluster (K-means); <b>soft<\/b> clustering lets an object belong to several (fuzzy c-means).<\/p>\n<p>Distance measures: Euclidean (q = 2) and Manhattan (q = 1) distances. <code>d = sqrt((x2 - x1)^2 + (y2 - y1)^2)<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h4>K-means (the teacher's method)<\/h4>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Decide K.<\/li><li>Take the K initial centroids (given in the question).<\/li><li>Calculate the distance of <b>every<\/b> point to every centroid.<\/li><li>Assign each point to its nearest centroid.<\/li><li>Calculate the new centre of each cluster as the mean of its points (all centroids are updated after all points are assigned).<\/li><li>Repeat 3 to 5 until the centres no longer change.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p><b>Layout for the answer:<\/b> one table per iteration (point, distance to each centroid, cluster), then the line for each new centroid with the sums shown, then the result.<\/p>\n<img class=\"fig\" src=\"img/kmeans_flow.png\" alt=\"K-means flow chart\">\n<h4>Choosing K: the elbow method<\/h4>\n<p>Plot the distortion (sum of squared distances) against K. It falls steeply and then flattens; the bend is the <b>elbow<\/b> and the best K (in the Nov24 graph, K = 3).<\/p>\n<p><b>Strengths:<\/b> simple, fast O(nkt), mutually exclusive clusters. <b>Weaknesses:<\/b> K fixed in advance, local optimum depending on the start, sensitive to outliers, not for non-convex shapes. <b>Applications:<\/b> market segmentation, targeted marketing, siting delivery centres, image segmentation.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q20a", "Nov24 Q8", "Nov24 Q21", "Apr26 Q9", "Apr26 Q14", "Apr26 Q20a", "Apr26 Q20b", "REV Q2", "KM Q1", "KM Q2", "KM Q3", "Apr24 Q9", "Apr24 Q22a", "Apr24 Q22b"]}, "4.7": {"h": "<p><b>Hierarchical clustering<\/b> organises data into a tree called a <b>dendrogram<\/b>. No need to fix the number of clusters in advance.<\/p>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th><\/th><th>Agglomerative (bottom-up)<\/th><th>Divisive (top-down)<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Start<\/td><td>each point is a cluster<\/td><td>all points in one cluster<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Step<\/td><td>merge the two closest<\/td><td>split a cluster<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Stop<\/td><td>one cluster<\/td><td>singletons<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<h4>Linkage criteria (distance between two clusters)<\/h4>\n<ul><li><b>Single<\/b>: minimum distance between members (most similar pair).<\/li><li><b>Complete<\/b>: maximum distance (most dissimilar pair).<\/li><li><b>Average<\/b>: average of all pairwise distances.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Single-linkage procedure (the one used in class)<\/h4>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Each point is its own cluster.<\/li><li>Compute the distance matrix (Euclidean for raw points).<\/li><li>Merge the closest pair; the merge height is that distance.<\/li><li><b>Update the matrix:<\/b> the new cluster's distance to every other cluster is the <b>minimum<\/b> of the two old distances.<\/li><li>Repeat until one cluster remains, then draw the dendrogram with the merge heights on the y-axis.<\/li><\/ol>\n<img class=\"fig\" src=\"img/n24_q15_dend.png\" alt=\"dendrogram example\">\n<p><b>Reading a dendrogram:<\/b> a horizontal cut line at some height; the number of vertical lines it crosses is the number of clusters (Nov25 Q8: 4). <b>Advantages:<\/b> easy, no K needed, gives an ordering. <b>Disadvantages:<\/b> a merge can never be undone, different distances give different trees, expensive for large data.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q8", "Nov25 Q14", "Nov24 Q9", "Nov24 Q15", "AGG Q1", "AGG Q2"]}, "4.8": {"h": "<p>A classifier is tested on unseen data and summarised in a <b>confusion matrix<\/b> (rows = actual class, columns = predicted class):<\/p>\n<div class=\"tbl scroll\"><table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Actual \\ Predicted<\/th><th>Positive<\/th><th>Negative<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Positive<\/td><td>TP<\/td><td>FN (Type II error)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Negative<\/td><td>FP (Type I error)<\/td><td>TN<\/td><\/tr><\/table><\/div>\n<ul><li><b>TP<\/b>: positive correctly predicted. <b>TN<\/b>: negative correctly predicted. <b>FP<\/b>: negative wrongly predicted positive. <b>FN<\/b>: positive wrongly predicted negative.<\/li><\/ul>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">Accuracy    = (TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN)\nRecall (sensitivity, TPR) = TP / (TP + FN)         of all real positives, how many were found\nPrecision   = TP / (TP + FP)                       of all predicted positives, how many are real\nSpecificity (TNR) = TN / (TN + FP)\nF1-score    = 2 x Precision x Recall / (Precision + Recall)      harmonic mean\nError rate  = (FP + FN) / total = 1 - accuracy<\/pre>\n<p>Accuracy can mislead on imbalanced data (a model that always says \"no cancer\" is 97% accurate if 3% have cancer but finds none): look at precision, recall and F1 too. Teacher's example: TP = 45, FN = 20, FP = 5, TN = 30 gives accuracy 75%, recall 69.2%, specificity 85.7%, precision 90%.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q21b", "Apr26 Q23b"]}, "5.1": {"h": "<p>A <b>recommender system<\/b> is a tool that predicts what a user will like and suggests it automatically. It is an application of machine learning that works on large numbers of items (movies, products, songs). Netflix and YouTube, Amazon and Flipkart, Facebook and LinkedIn all use it.<\/p>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th><\/th><th>Search engine<\/th><th>Recommender system<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Type<\/td><td>pull: the user asks<\/td><td>push: the system suggests<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Works on<\/td><td>keywords of the query<\/td><td>past preferences<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Purpose<\/td><td>information retrieval<\/td><td>information discovery<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<h4>Why needed, and data used<\/h4>\n<ul><li>Too much information; less effort for the user; more sales, engagement and retention; discovery of new items.<\/li><li><b>Explicit<\/b> data: ratings, comments, likes. <b>Implicit<\/b> data: searches, purchase history, clicks. Also item details (keywords, categories) and user details (profile), and user-item interactions.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Content-based filtering: \"show me more of what I liked\"<\/h4>\n<p>Does not use other users. It describes every <b>item<\/b> (genre, artist, keywords) and the user <b>profile<\/b> (from the items liked), then recommends the items most similar to the profile.<\/p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Describe each item as a vector (genre flags or TF-IDF of words).<\/li><li>Build the user profile.<\/li><li>Compute <b>cosine similarity<\/b> between the profile and the items.<\/li><li>Recommend the highest.<\/li><\/ol>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"gbox\">TF = word count in document / total words      IDF = log(total documents / documents containing the word)\nTF-IDF = TF x IDF          (words in every document, like &quot;the&quot;, get IDF = 0)\nsim(a, b) = Σ(ai bi) / ( √(Σ ai²) x √(Σ bi²) )      1 = same direction, 0 = nothing in common<\/pre>\n<p><b>Advantages:<\/b> no other users needed, works for new items, easy to explain. <b>Limitations:<\/b> only similar items (over-specialisation), needs good item features, new-user cold start.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q9", "Nov24 Q10", "Nov24 Q24a", "Apr26 Q10", "Apr26 Q16", "Apr26 Q22a", "Apr26 Q22b", "Apr26 Q23a", "Apr26 Q24b", "Apr24 Q16", "Apr24 Q23a", "Apr24 Q24"]}, "5.2": {"h": "<p><b>Collaborative filtering:<\/b> \"tell me what is popular among people like me\". It uses only the user-item ratings (the <b>utility matrix<\/b>, mostly empty, 0 = not rated) and does not need to understand the items. <b>User-based:<\/b> compare users. <b>Item-based:<\/b> compare items.<\/p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Build the utility matrix.<\/li><li>Compute the similarity between users (cosine or Pearson).<\/li><li>Pick the K most similar users who rated the item.<\/li><li>Predict the rating: the average of their ratings, or the weighted <code>R = sum(sim x rating) / sum(sim)<\/code>; recommend the highest predictions.<\/li><\/ol>\n<p><b>Pearson (centred cosine):<\/b> some users rate high and some low, so subtract each user's average rating before the cosine; a result of 0 means nothing in common and a negative value means opposite tastes.<\/p>\n<p>Teacher's example: users A (4, 0, 0, 5, 1, 0, 0) and B (5, 5, 4, 0, 0, 0, 0): dot product = 20, |A| = sqrt 42, |B| = sqrt 66, so cosine = 20 / (6.48 x 8.12) = 0.38.<\/p>\n<p><b>Limits:<\/b> needs a large community, the cold-start problem for new users and items, sparsity of the utility matrix.<\/p>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th><\/th><th>Content-based<\/th><th>Collaborative<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Based on<\/td><td>features of items<\/td><td>ratings of other users<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Other users<\/td><td>not needed<\/td><td>needed<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>New item<\/td><td>works<\/td><td>cold start<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Weakness<\/td><td>suggests only similar items<\/td><td>similar users may still differ<\/td><\/tr><\/table>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q23a", "Nov24 Q24b"]}, "5.3": {"h": "<h4>Hybrid recommenders: seven ways to combine<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Method<\/th><th>How it works<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Weighted<\/td><td>add both scores with fixed weights, a x CF + (1 - a) x CBF<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Switching<\/td><td>choose one method for the situation<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Mixed<\/td><td>show the results of both together<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Cascade<\/td><td>one ranks, the other refines or breaks ties<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Feature combination<\/td><td>features of one feed the other's model<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Feature augmentation<\/td><td>the output of one becomes an input of the main one<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Meta-level<\/td><td>the model learned by one becomes the input of the other<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Example: a hotel recommender uses content-based filtering (price, location, amenities) for a new guest and adds collaborative filtering (ratings of similar guests) for a returning guest. Benefits: higher accuracy, more variety, better cold-start handling.<\/p>\n<h4>Evaluating a recommender<\/h4>\n<ul><li><b>Prediction error:<\/b> MAE = (1/n) sum |actual - predicted|; RMSE = sqrt((1/n) sum (actual - predicted)^2) (punishes big errors more). Lower is better.<\/li><li><b>Top-N quality:<\/b> hit rate = hits / users; ARHR = average of 1/rank of the hits (top positions earn more credit).<\/li><li><b>Beyond accuracy:<\/b> coverage (share of items that can be recommended), diversity = 1 - average similarity of the recommended items, novelty (how unpopular the items are).<\/li><\/ul>\n<p>Explicit data (ratings) vs implicit data (clicks, purchases, watch time): explicit is accurate but scarce, implicit is plentiful but noisy.<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q15", "Nov25 Q23b", "Nov24 Q16"]}, "6.1": {"h": "<p><b>Ethics in data science<\/b> is the set of principles that govern how data is handled, to ensure fairness, transparency and accountability and to avoid misuse. The three core pillars are <b>fairness<\/b>, <b>accountability<\/b> and <b>transparency<\/b>.<\/p>\n<h4>Ethical frameworks and principles<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Framework<\/th><th>Idea<\/th><th>Example<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Utilitarianism<\/td><td>maximise well-being of the greatest number<\/td><td>using location data to control an outbreak despite some loss of privacy<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Deontological<\/td><td>follow moral duties and rules whatever the consequence<\/td><td>refusing to collect data without explicit consent even if it would improve the service<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Virtue ethics<\/td><td>moral character: honesty, fairness, compassion<\/td><td>a developer refusing to build facial recognition for surveillance<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Rights-based / care-based<\/td><td>protect individual rights; care for vulnerable people<\/td><td>privacy and autonomy rights<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Principles: <b>beneficence<\/b> (do good), <b>non-maleficence<\/b> (avoid harm), <b>autonomy<\/b> (respect choices), <b>justice<\/b> (fair burdens and benefits), plus fidelity, integrity, respect and responsibility.<\/p>\n<h4>Privacy and confidentiality<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th><\/th><th>Privacy<\/th><th>Confidentiality<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Meaning<\/td><td>the individual's right to control their personal data<\/td><td>protection of sensitive data once shared, against unauthorised disclosure<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Stage<\/td><td>before data is shared<\/td><td>after data is shared<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Responsibility<\/td><td>mainly the individual<\/td><td>mainly the data holder<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Breach example<\/td><td>tracking without consent<\/td><td>leaking medical records<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Types of privacy: informational, physical, decisional, proprietary. Loss of privacy leads to identity theft, profiling, discrimination and distress.<\/p>\n<h4>Data collection and consent<\/h4>\n<ul><li><b>Informed consent:<\/b> people must clearly understand what is collected and how it is used (opt-in, plain language). Issues: secondary use or selling to third parties, misleading terms, unrelated permissions.<\/li><li><b>GDPR principles:<\/b> purpose limitation, data minimisation, lawful basis, transparency and fairness, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity and confidentiality. <b>HIPAA<\/b> governs US medical data.<\/li><li><b>The 5 C's:<\/b> consent, confidentiality, control, compliance, communication.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Anonymisation and de-identification<\/h4>\n<ul><li><b>Anonymisation<\/b> removes all personal identifiers so the data cannot be traced back (irreversible). <b>De-identification<\/b> removes or masks identifiers but can still be reversed by data matching.<\/li><li>Techniques: <b>suppression<\/b> (remove zip codes), <b>generalisation<\/b> (age 34 becomes 30-39), <b>noise addition<\/b>, <b>differential privacy<\/b> (statistical noise so no individual is exposed), k-anonymity, encryption, access control.<\/li><li><b>Re-identification (linkage attack):<\/b> joining \"anonymous\" data with public data to identify people; this is the usual twist of the case-study questions.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>How to answer a case-study question (12 marks)<\/h4>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Name the data and the stakeholders from the case.<\/li><li>Analyse each concern asked for (privacy, confidentiality, consent, re-identification, transparency) and tie it to a fact in the case.<\/li><li>Ethical measures: opt-in consent, minimisation, purpose limitation, policy, audit, breach notification, compliance.<\/li><li>Technical measures: anonymisation (k-anonymity), differential privacy, encryption, access control, aggregation, retention limit.<\/li><li>Close with the 5 C's.<\/li><\/ol>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q24", "ETH Q1", "ETH Q2", "ETH Q3", "ETH Q4"]}, "6.2": {"h": "<p><b>Bias<\/b> is a systematic error that leads to unfair outcomes. Types:<\/p>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Type<\/th><th>Meaning and example<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Historical<\/td><td>inherited discrimination in the data (past hiring biases)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Representation<\/td><td>under-representation of a group (voice assistants failing on regional accents)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Measurement<\/td><td>inaccurate proxies (zip code standing for income)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Algorithmic<\/td><td>from the model design or learning (credit scores penalising certain zip codes)<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p><b>Fairness<\/b> means treating individuals and groups equitably, without discrimination on protected attributes (race, gender, age, religion, disability).<\/p>\n<ul><li><b>Group fairness (demographic parity):<\/b> the approval rate is the same for each group.<\/li><li><b>Individual fairness:<\/b> two applicants with the same credit history and income get the same decision, whatever their gender or race.<\/li><li><b>Equal opportunity:<\/b> among those who would repay, the same share of each group is approved.<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Mitigating bias in ML models<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th>Stage<\/th><th>Actions<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Pre-processing (data)<\/td><td>diverse data collection, reweighing or oversampling, find and mask proxy features<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>In-processing (training)<\/td><td>fairness constraints in the objective, adversarial debiasing<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Post-processing (deployment)<\/td><td>continuous audit and monitoring, human in the loop for high-stakes decisions, explainable AI<\/td><\/tr><\/table>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q10"]}, "6.3": {"h": "<ul><li><b>Transparency<\/b> builds trust and allows audit: documentation (datasheets and model cards), open access to datasets and models, and clear communication of model decisions to affected users, especially in healthcare and finance.<\/li><li><b>Accountability<\/b> is the obligation of people and organisations to take ultimate responsibility for the outcomes, good and bad, of the AI systems they build and deploy. Three functions: <b>assigning responsibility<\/b> (who), <b>enabling auditability<\/b> (how: the process can be examined afterwards) and <b>providing redress and recourse<\/b> (the fix when a decision is unfair or harmful).<\/li><\/ul>\n<h4>Explainability and interpretability<\/h4>\n<table class=\"work\"><tr><th><\/th><th>Explainability<\/th><th>Interpretability<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Meaning<\/td><td>ability to describe <i>why<\/i> a model made a specific prediction<\/td><td>simplicity of the model's structure so a non-expert can follow it<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Example<\/td><td>a health model explaining the likelihood of a disease from the inputs<\/td><td>a decision tree for credit scoring with clear approval rules<\/td><\/tr><\/table>\n<p>Techniques: <b>LIME<\/b> (explains a single prediction of a complex model by approximating it with a simple one), <b>SHAP<\/b> (feature importance for any model), decision trees (inherently interpretable). Trade-off: high-accuracy models such as deep learning are often hard to interpret, which makes them hard to justify in critical decisions.<\/p>\n<p>The FAT flow framework applies these across the stages: data gathering (privacy, consent), pre-processing (re-identification, fair relabelling), modelling (discrimination-aware, explainable), evaluation (honest reporting, no p-hacking) and deployment (governance, unintended consequences).<\/p>", "qs": ["Nov25 Q16"]}};
